sion.\n# (L-L1)/phi = lam^2*(-2c^2*px^2) - lam*lap\n# = -(1/2)*c^2/(4(x+1)) + (1/2)*(1/(1+t)^2 + c^2/(4(x+1)^(3/2)))\n# = 1/(2(1+t)^2) + c^2*(1/(8(x+1)^(3/2)) - 1/(8(x+1)))\n# = 1/(2(1+t)^2) + c^2/(8(x+1)) * (1/sqrt(x+1) - 1)\n# For x >= 0: sqrt(x+1) >= 1, so 1/sqrt(x+1) <= 1, so (1/sqrt(x+1)-1) <= 0.\n# So the c^2 correction is <= 0, and the expression might go negative for large c.\n\n# Max of |correction|: \n# f(x) = 1/(8(x+1)) * (1 - 1/sqrt(x+1)) \n# f(0) = 0\n# f -> 0 as x -> inf\n# f'(x) = ... has a maximum somewhere.\n# Let u = x+1 >= 1:\n# f = (1-1/sqrt(u))/(8u)\n# f' = d/du[(1-u^(-1/2))/(8u)] = [(1/2)u^(-3/2)*8u - 8*(1-u^(-1/2))]/(64u^2)\n# = [4u^(-1/2) - 8 + 8u^(-1/2)]/(64u^2) = [12u^(-1/2) - 8]/(64u^2)\n# = 0 when 12/sqrt(u) = 8, sqrt(u) = 3/2, u = 9/4, x = 5/4.\n# f(5/4) = (1-2/3)/(8*9/4) = (1/3)/18 = 1/54\n\n# So max |correction| = c^2/54.\n# Condition 1a: 1/(2(1+t)^2) - c^2/54 > 0 needs t^2 < 27/c^2 - 1.\n# For c=2: t^2 < 27/4 - 1 = 5.75, t < 2.4. Fails for t > 2.4!\n\nprint(\"PROBLEM: Condition 1a fails for large t and c.\")\nprint(\"At x=5/4, the correction is c^2/54.\")\nprint(\"Need 1/(2(1+t)^2) > c^2/54, i.e., t < sqrt(27/c^2 - 1)\")\nprint(\"For c=2: t < 2.4. For c=1: t < 5.1. For c=0.5: t < 10.3\")\nprint()\nprint(\"The c=2 case is problematic.\")\nprint()\n\n# The issue: alpha=1 makes L/phi = lam^2*tsq, which doesn't involve alpha.\n# With alpha ≠ 1, L/phi = lam^2*tsq - (alpha-1)*lam*sq\n# (L-L1)/phi = lam^2*(-2c^2*px^2) - (alpha-1)*lam*sq - lam*lap\n# = lam^2*(-2c^2*px^2) + |lam|*((alpha-1)*sq + lap)\n# = |lam|*((alpha-1)*sq + lap - |lam|*2c^2*px^2)\n# = |lam|*((alpha-1)*sq + ptt + c^2*pxx - 2c^2*lam^2/(4(x+1))*|lam|)\n# Hmm, this is getting messy. Let me use alpha to help.\n\n# sq = ptt - c^2*pxx = 1/(1+t)^2 - c^2/(4(x+1)^(3/2))\n# lap = ptt + c^2*pxx = 1/(1+t)^2 + c^2/(4(x+1)^(3/2))\n\n# (alpha-1)*sq + lap = (alpha-1)*(1/(1+t)^2 - c^2/(4(x+1)^(3/2))) + 1/(1+t)^2 + c^2/(4(x+1)^(3/2))\n# = alpha/(1+t)^2 + (2-alpha)*c^2/(4(x+1)^(3/2))\n\n# So (L-L1)/phi = |lam|*(alpha/(1+t)^2 + (2-alpha)*c^2/(4(x+1)^(3/2)) - 2c^2*|lam|/(4(x+1)))\n# = |lam|*(alpha/(1+t)^2 + c^2/4 * ((2-alpha)/(x+1)^(3/2) - 2|lam|/(x+1)))\n# \n# For alpha=2: simplifies to\n# |lam|*(2/(1+t)^2 + c^2/4 * (-2|lam|/(x+1)))\n# = |lam|*(2/(1+t)^2 - c^2*|lam|/(2(x+1)))\n# At t=0: 2 - c^2*|lam|/(2(x+1)). For x=0: 2 - c^2*|lam|/2. \n# For |lam|=1/2, c=2: 2 - 4/4 = 1 > 0. ✓\n# For x=0, t->inf: 0 - c^2/4 < 0. BAD.\n\n# For alpha slightly > 1: the term (2-alpha)/(x+1)^(3/2) is positive and helps.\n# We need alpha/(1+t)^2 + c^2*(2-alpha)/(4(x+1)^(3/2)) > 2c^2*|lam|/(4(x+1))\n# As t->inf: c^2*(2-alpha)/(4(x+1)^(3/2)) > c^2*|lam|/(2(x+1))\n# i.e., (2-alpha)/(x+1)^(3/2) > 2|lam|/(x+1)\n# i.e., (2-alpha)/sqrt(x+1) > 2|lam|\n# This fails as x->inf! The LHS -> 0.\n\n# Hmm. So as (x,t) -> (inf, inf), the leading behavior of (L-L1)/phi is\n# determined by the x-independent t-dependent term: alpha/(1+t)^2.\n# This is always > 0 ✓ (just small for large t).\n# The c^2 correction term goes to 0 as x->inf.\n# So (L-L1)/phi -> alpha/(1+t)^2 > 0 as x->inf. ✓\n\n# The problem is at INTERMEDIATE x where the c^2 correction is most negative.\n# The correction is: c^2/4 * ((2-alpha)/(x+1)^(3/2) - 2|lam|/(x+1))\n# = c^2/(4(x+1)) * ((2-alpha)/sqrt(x+1) - 2|lam|)\n# This is negative when sqrt(x+1) > (2-alpha)/(2|lam|).\n# i.e., x > ((2-alpha)/(2|lam|))^2 - 1.\n# For alpha=1, |lam|=1/2: x > (1/1)^2 - 1 = 0. So negative for all x > 0!\n\n# Max absolute value of correction:\n# d/dx [1/(x+1) * (2|lam| - (2-alpha)/sqrt(x+1))] at the optimum...\n# This is getting complicated. Let me just try different alpha values numerically.\n\n# Actually, let me try alpha = 3/2 with lam = -1/2:\n# (L-L1)/phi = |lam|*((3/2)/(1+t)^2 + c^2/4*((1/2)/(x+1)^(3/2) - 1/(x+1)))\n# = (1/2)*((3/2)/(1+t)^2 + c^2*(1/(8(x+1)^(3/2)) - 1/(4(x+1))))\n# = 3/(4(1+t)^2) + c^2*(1/(16(x+1)^(3/2)) - 1/(8(x+1)))\n# = 3/(4(1+t)^2) + c^2/(16(x+1)) * (1/sqrt(x+1) - 2)\n# For x >= 0: 1/sqrt(x+1) <= 1 < 2, so the c^2 term is